Personal Experiences

Maria, 34 – Flu
“I got the flu last winter, and it honestly knocked me out more than I expected. It started with just feeling tired and a little cold, but by the next day, I had a fever, and my whole body ached. I remember not even wanting to get out of bed because everything felt heavy. What really helped me was just slowing down and letting my body rest, even though I felt like I was wasting time. I drank a lot of water and some soup, even when I didn't have much of an appetite. I also stayed home from work for a few days because I didn't want to get my coworkers sick. After about 3 or 4 days, the fever went down, but I still felt weak for a while after. It kinda reminded me that pushing through being sick isn't always the best idea.”

James, 17 – Stomach Virus
“I had a stomach virus a few months ago, and it was honestly one of the worst things I’ve dealt with. It came out of nowhere, like I felt fine at school, and then later that night, I started feeling really sick. I couldn't keep food down, and I just felt really weak and tired. I mostly stayed in my room and tried to sleep it off, but it was hard. My mom kept telling me to drink small sips of water so I wouldn't get dehydrated, and that actually helped a bit. I also had to be careful about washing my hands and not touching stuff around the house too much, so no one else got it. It lasted about 2 days, but felt longer. After it passed, I was just really careful about what I ate for a few days.”

Tanya, 42 – COVID-19
“When I tested positive for COVID-19, I was honestly nervous because I had heard so many different stories from people. For me, it started with a mild cough and feeling really tired, but then I noticed I couldn't smell or taste anything, which was kinda scary. I made sure to stay isolated in my room so I didn't spread it to my family. If I had to come out, I would wear a mask and wipe down things I touched. I kept checking my temperature and tried to rest as much as possible. Drinking warm drinks helped a little with the cough. It took about a week before I started feeling better, but even after that, I didn't feel fully normal for a bit. That whole experience made me take sickness more seriously than before.”

Luis, 28 – Common Cold
“I get the common cold probably more than I should, and over time I’ve kinda figured out what works for me. Usually it starts with a sore throat and runny nose, then I just feel kinda off for a few days. It's not super serious, but it still slows me down a lot. I try to drink hot tea, especially at night, and get more sleep than usual. One thing I try to be more aware of now is not being too close to people when I know I'm getting sick, because it's easy to pass it on without thinking. I also wash my hands more often during that time. It usually clears up in a few days, but I try not to ignore it because it can still affect other people.”


Aaliyah, 15 – Strep Throat
“When I had strep throat, it was really painful, like way worse than just a normal sore throat. It hurt to talk, swallow, and even drink water sometimes. I remember feeling really frustrated because I couldn't really do anything and had to stay home from school. My parents took me to the doctor, and I got medicine, which helped after a couple of days. I also made sure not to share drinks or food with anyone because I didn't want my family to get sick, too. I spent most of my time resting and watching shows because I didn't have the energy to do much else. After a few days, it started getting better, but it definitely made me more careful about things like germs and sharing stuff."
